<!-- CHAPTER 1 -->
<h1>1. INTRODUCTION</h1>

<h2>1.1 Overview of the Project</h2>
<p>The <b>Employee Leave Management System (ELMS)</b> is an automated intranet web application designed to handle all aspects of employee leave administration within corporate and institutional setups. In modern workplace environments, managing human resources and workforce availability is vital to organizational productivity, customer satisfaction, and operational continuity.</p>

<p>The ELMS replaces slow, error-prone manual paper trails and unstructured email correspondence with an automated, secure web workflow. Built entirely using modern <b>Java Servlet technology</b> on the backend and direct HTML streaming via Java presentation classes on the client tier, the application ensures high reliability, clean architecture, and rapid response times without reliance on heavy client-side JavaScript frameworks or legacy JavaServer Pages (JSP).</p>

<h2>1.2 Problem Statement</h2>
<p>Traditional leave processing within organizations involves physical paper application forms, manual signatures from managers, verbal requests, or unstructured email exchanges. This approach introduces significant problems:</p>
<p class="no-indent">
1. <b>Time Consumption:</b> Applications take days to route between employees, department heads, and human resource personnel.<br>
2. <b>Lack of Transparency:</b> Employees have no real-time insight into the review status of their applications or reasons for refusal.<br>
3. <b>Record Inaccuracy:</b> Manual ledger entries are prone to calculation errors, missing dates, duplicate approvals, and lost papers.<br>
4. <b>Administrative Overhead:</b> HR managers must manually calculate leave balances, compile department availability sheets, and maintain audit trails.
</p>

<h2>1.3 Objectives</h2>
<p>The principal objectives of the Employee Leave Management System are:</p>
<p class="no-indent">
• <b>Centralized Authentication:</b> Provide secure user registration, credential validation, and role-based session routing for Employees and Administrators.<br>
• <b>Categorized Leave Application:</b> Allow employees to submit leave requests specifying leave type (Casual, Sick, Earned, Maternity, Paternity, Unpaid), date boundaries, and detailed justifications.<br>
• <b>Real-Time Tracking & Badging:</b> Offer an intuitive dashboard where employees monitor leave progression through visual status indicators (<i>Pending</i>, <i>Approved</i>, <i>Rejected</i>).<br>
• <b>Administrative Decision Workflow:</b> Empower administrators with interactive dashboards to inspect employee requests, make one-click approval or rejection decisions, and attach explanatory remarks.<br>
• <b>Auditability & Cancellation:</b> Permit employees to cancel unresolved pending leaves while maintaining historical logs of all processed leaves.<br>
• <b>Directory & Resource Oversight:</b> Enable management to review company-wide employee directories, department distributions, and collective leave statistics.
</p>

<h2>1.4 Scope</h2>
<p>The project encompasses:</p>
<p class="no-indent">
• Core employee self-service modules: account creation, login, session termination, dashboard metrics, leave application forms, and historical leave tracking.<br>
• Administrative management modules: centralized executive dashboard, pending leave action panels, historical leave review, decision remarks recording, and staff directory inspection.<br>
• Server-side data validation, relational persistence via JDBC, automatic database schema bootstrapping, and cross-site scripting (XSS) prevention.<br>
• Out of scope: Third-party payroll integrations, automated email/SMS push gateways, biometric punch-clock synchronization, and enterprise single sign-on (SSO) protocols.
</p>

<h2>1.5 Feasibility Study</h2>
<p><b>Technical Feasibility:</b> The platform is built on enterprise-grade, established technologies: Core Java (JDK 17+), Jakarta Servlet API 6.0, JDBC, MySQL 8.0, Apache Maven, and Apache Tomcat 10.1. All components run stably on standard operating systems and require minimal hardware footprints, rendering the system technically robust.</p>

<p><b>Economic Feasibility:</b> The complete software stack comprises open-source and free technologies—Java SE, Apache Tomcat, MySQL Community Server, and Maven. Development, deployment, and testing require no commercial software licenses, making the system cost-effective.</p>

<p><b>Operational Feasibility:</b> The user interface adheres to clean, ergonomic, and uncluttered design standards. Forms feature intuitive dropdowns, HTML5 date selectors, and clear action buttons. Users with basic web browsing skills can navigate the system effortlessly.</p>

<p><b>Schedule Feasibility:</b> The modular design of the application allowed iterative development divided into distinct milestones: database modeling, DAO implementation, authentication servlets, employee portals, administrative review workflows, testing, and documentation, ensuring completion well within academic deadlines.</p>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- CHAPTER 2 -->
<h1>2. TECHNOLOGIES USED</h1>

<h2>2.1 HTML and Embedded Lightweight CSS</h2>
<p>The user interface is generated through clean HTML markup rendered directly by server-side Java presentation components (<code>HtmlTemplate.java</code>). Rather than loading heavy external CSS frameworks such as Bootstrap, Tailwind, or complex JavaScript bundles that increase page weight and network latency, the application uses an optimized, embedded CSS styling strategy:</p>
<p class="no-indent">
• Clean typography utilizing modern system font stacks (<code>'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif</code>).<br>
• Consistent color palettes conveying authority and hierarchy (navy headers <code>#1e293b</code>, royal blue accents <code>#2563eb</code>, green success indicators <code>#16a34a</code>, amber warnings <code>#d97706</code>, and red alerts <code>#dc2626</code>).<br>
• Responsive, flexible layouts using CSS Flexbox for metric cards, forms, and data tables.<br>
• Zero client-side external asset dependencies, ensuring immediate page load speeds and full offline intranet capability.
</p>

<h2>2.2 Core Java (JDK 17+)</h2>
<p>Java serves as the foundation for all server-side application logic. Its strong typing, object-oriented principles, exception handling, and platform independence make it ideal for transactional enterprise web systems. Java 17+ LTS language features ensure high performance, security, and long-term maintainability.</p>

<h2>2.3 Jakarta Servlets (Servlet API 6.0)</h2>
<p>Jakarta Servlets act as controller components within the application. Servlets map incoming HTTP requests (<code>GET</code> and <code>POST</code>) to business logic methods, manage user sessions via <code>HttpSession</code>, validate submitted form parameters, coordinate data retrieval with DAO classes, and stream sanitized HTML markup back to the client browser. The project adheres to Servlet API 6.0 specifications compatible with modern application servers.</p>

<h2>2.4 Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) and MySQL</h2>
<p>Data persistence is handled by <b>MySQL 8.0</b> connected through the official <b>MySQL Connector/J 8.4.0</b> driver using standard Java Database Connectivity (JDBC):</p>
<p class="no-indent">
• <b>PreparedStatements:</b> All SQL queries use parameterized <code>PreparedStatement</code> interfaces, completely preventing SQL Injection vulnerabilities.<br>
• <b>Relational Integrity:</b> Foreign key constraints with cascading rules link leave requests to employee identities.<br>
• <b>Dual-Engine Architecture:</b> The data layer supports native MySQL 8.0 as the primary production database while retaining transparent fallback compatibility with embedded H2 databases.
</p>

<h2>2.5 Apache Maven and Apache Tomcat 10.1</h2>
<p><b>Apache Maven 3.9:</b> Serves as the build automation and dependency management tool, assembling compiled classes, web configuration files, and JDBC libraries into a standard Web Application Archive (<code>leavemanagement.war</code>).</p>
<p><b>Apache Tomcat 10.1:</b> An open-source, high-performance web server and servlet container implementing Jakarta Servlet 6.0 specifications, hosting the deployed application.</p>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- CHAPTER 3 -->
<h1>3. SYSTEM ANALYSIS AND DESIGN</h1>

<h2>3.1 Functional Requirements</h2>
<h3>Employee Module:</h3>
<p class="no-indent">
1. <b>User Registration:</b> Visitors can register with their full name, email address, secure password, and department.<br>
2. <b>User Login & Session Management:</b> Authenticated employees gain access to personal dashboards with session timeouts and secure logout.<br>
3. <b>Leave Application:</b> Employees can apply for leaves by selecting leave types, choosing valid start and end dates, and supplying explanations.<br>
4. <b>Dashboard Summaries:</b> Employees view live counters for Pending, Approved, and Rejected requests alongside recent submissions.<br>
5. <b>Leave Tracking & History:</b> Employees can inspect the complete history of their leaves, view approval badges, and read administrative feedback.<br>
6. <b>Application Cancellation:</b> Employees can cancel their own unresolved <i>Pending</i> requests with one click.
</p>

<h3>Administrator Module:</h3>
<p class="no-indent">
1. <b>Executive Overview:</b> Administrators view organizational statistics: total registered staff, pending approvals, approved leaves, and rejected counts.<br>
2. <b>Pending Request Review:</b> An action panel displays all pending leaves with immediate <b>Approve</b> and <b>Reject</b> buttons and custom remarks input fields.<br>
3. <b>Company-Wide Leave Audit:</b> Administrators can browse, sort, and inspect all leave records across the organization.<br>
4. <b>Employee Directory:</b> Administrators can browse all registered personnel, departmental distributions, and cumulative leave tallies.
</p>

<h2>3.2 Non-Functional Requirements</h2>
<p><b>Security:</b> Strict separation of privileges between Employees and Admins; parameterized SQL queries to prevent SQL Injection; HTML escaping across all dynamic fields to eliminate Cross-Site Scripting (XSS); secure session invalidation.</p>
<p><b>Usability:</b> Clean visual presentation with clear status badges, responsive tabular layouts, and intuitive form controls requiring no user manuals.</p>
<p><b>Performance:</b> Direct servlet HTML rendering without JSP translation overhead guarantees sub-second page response times.</p>
<p><b>Reliability:</b> Automated schema generation and self-healing database initialization ensure zero deployment downtime.</p>
<p><b>Portability:</b> Standard Java EE WAR packaging guarantees immediate deployment to any compliant servlet container across Windows, Linux, or cloud environments.</p>

<h2>3.3 System Architecture</h2>
<p>The application employs a layered client-server architecture based on Model-View-Controller (MVC) principles:</p>

<div class="code-block">
[ Web Browser (Client Presentation Tier) ]
                 │
                 ▼  HTTP Requests (GET / POST)
[ Jakarta Servlet Controllers (Controller Tier) ]
   ├── LoginServlet / RegisterServlet / LogoutServlet
   ├── DashboardServlet / ApplyLeaveServlet / LeaveHistoryServlet
   └── AdminDashboardServlet / ManageLeavesServlet / EmployeesServlet
                 │
                 ▼  Business Logic & Parameter Validation
[ Model & Utility Tier ]
   ├── Employee.java / LeaveRequest.java (Entity Models)
   └── HtmlTemplate.java (Presentation Utility / Sanitization)
                 │
                 ▼  JDBC Calls (PreparedStatement)
[ Data Access Tier (DatabaseHelper.java) ]
                 │
                 ▼  SQL Queries / ResultSets
[ MySQL 8.0 Relational Database (leave_db) ]
</div>

<h2>3.4 Database Design</h2>
<p>The relational schema comprises two core normalized entities in third normal form (3NF):</p>

<h3>1. <code>employees</code> Table</h3>
<table>
    <tr>
        <th>Column Name</th>
        <th>Data Type</th>
        <th>Constraints</th>
        <th>Description</th>
    </tr>
    <tr><td><code>id</code></td><td>INT</td><td>PRIMARY KEY, AUTO_INCREMENT</td><td>Unique employee identifier</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>VARCHAR(100)</td><td>NOT NULL</td><td>Full name of the employee</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>email</code></td><td>VARCHAR(100)</td><td>UNIQUE, NOT NULL</td><td>Login email address</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>password</code></td><td>VARCHAR(100)</td><td>NOT NULL</td><td>Account authentication password</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>department</code></td><td>VARCHAR(50)</td><td>NOT NULL</td><td>Assigned organizational department</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>role</code></td><td>VARCHAR(20)</td><td>DEFAULT 'employee'</td><td>Access role (<code>admin</code> or <code>employee</code>)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>2. <code>leave_requests</code> Table</h3>
<table>
    <tr>
        <th>Column Name</th>
        <th>Data Type</th>
        <th>Constraints</th>
        <th>Description</th>
    </tr>
    <tr><td><code>id</code></td><td>INT</td><td>PRIMARY KEY, AUTO_INCREMENT</td><td>Unique leave request identifier</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>employee_id</code></td><td>INT</td><td>NOT NULL, FK &rarr; employees(id)</td><td>Associated employee reference</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>leave_type</code></td><td>VARCHAR(30)</td><td>NOT NULL</td><td>Type of leave (Casual, Sick, etc.)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>start_date</code></td><td>DATE</td><td>NOT NULL</td><td>Beginning date of leave</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>end_date</code></td><td>DATE</td><td>NOT NULL</td><td>Concluding date of leave</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>reason</code></td><td>VARCHAR(500)</td><td>NOT NULL</td><td>Employee justification</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>status</code></td><td>VARCHAR(20)</td><td>DEFAULT 'Pending'</td><td>Current status (Pending, Approved, Rejected)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>applied_date</code></td><td>DATE</td><td>DEFAULT CURRENT_DATE</td><td>Date on which leave was submitted</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>admin_remarks</code></td><td>VARCHAR(300)</td><td>NULL</td><td>Explanatory note from administrator</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>3.5 Use-Case Description</h2>
<p><b>Primary Actors:</b></p>
<p class="no-indent">
• <b>Employee:</b> An authenticated staff member who applies for leaves, monitors approval statuses, and manages personal requests.<br>
• <b>Administrator:</b> A privileged manager responsible for reviewing leave applications, approving or rejecting requests, and auditing workforce availability.
</p>

<p><b>Main Scenarios:</b></p>
<p class="no-indent">
• <i>Employee Applies for Leave:</i> Employee logs in &rarr; selects "Apply Leave" &rarr; specifies leave type, date range, and reason &rarr; system validates date order &rarr; request saved with status <code>Pending</code>.<br>
• <i>Administrator Reviews Leave:</i> Administrator logs in &rarr; opens Admin Dashboard &rarr; views pending request &rarr; enters optional remarks &rarr; clicks "Approve" or "Reject" &rarr; database updates status and remarks &rarr; employee dashboard reflects decision immediately.<br>
• <i>Employee Cancels Leave:</i> Employee opens Leave History &rarr; identifies a <code>Pending</code> request &rarr; clicks "Cancel" &rarr; system verifies ownership and status &rarr; record deleted safely.
</p>

