<!-- CHAPTER 7 -->
<h1>7. CONCLUSION AND FUTURE ENHANCEMENTS</h1>

<h2>7.1 Conclusion</h2>
<p>The <b>Employee Leave Management System (ELMS)</b> successfully demonstrates an automated, secure, and user-friendly web solution for corporate and academic absence tracking. By utilizing pure <b>Java Servlets (Jakarta Servlet API 6.0)</b>, direct dynamic HTML streaming via Java classes, and <b>JDBC with MySQL 8.0</b>, the application achieves exceptional response performance and structural clarity without relying on legacy JSP files or bulky external CSS frameworks.</p>

<p>The system delivers distinct, role-based workflows for employees and administrators, eliminating paper handling, expediting decision turnarounds, and maintaining transparent, audit-ready records. The project satisfies all academic objectives of the Skill Oriented Course and stands as a complete, practical enterprise software implementation.</p>

<h2>7.2 Future Enhancements</h2>
<p class="no-indent">
1. <b>Automated Notifications:</b> Integration of JavaMail API to send real-time email notifications to employees upon approval or rejection of their leaves.<br>
2. <b>Leave Balance Quotas:</b> Implementation of annual leave quotas (e.g., 12 Casual Leaves, 10 Sick Leaves per year) with automatic balance deductions upon approval.<br>
3. <b>Document Attachment:</b> Enabling file uploads allowing employees to attach medical certificates or supporting documents for sick or maternity leave requests.<br>
4. <b>Calendar Visualization:</b> An interactive monthly organizational calendar displaying approved leaves to prevent department understaffing.<br>
5. <b>Password Hashing (jBCrypt):</b> Upgrading plaintext database passwords to cryptographic hash digests (e.g., BCrypt / Argon2) with salt generation.<br>
6. <b>PDF Report Generation:</b> Allowing HR administrators to export monthly absence summaries and audit logs directly to formatted PDF or Excel documents.
</p>

